Claims:
[
    "Vibe testing is the practice of giving an LLM‑based application just a handful of prompts—usually five to ten—reading the answers, and then deciding “by feel” whether the system seems to be working.",
    "It’s an informal, subjective check: you ask a few questions that come to mind, see if the responses look right, and if they do, you conclude the app is fine.",
    "Because the set of prompts is small, chosen ad‑hoc, and you rely on your personal impression, the process isn’t systematic or repeatable; the next time you might ask completely different questions and get a different sense of quality.",
    "That casual approach is useful only for quick, personal experiments.",
    "It falls short for a production‑grade deployment because it doesn’t give you any guarantees about the model’s behavior across the wide variety of inputs it will encounter in the real world.",
    "Since vibe testing is subjective and not repeatable, you can’t reliably detect edge cases, biases, failures, or performance regressions.",
    "In an industry setting you need a more rigorous, methodological evaluation—structured test suites, quantitative metrics, and repeatable procedures—to ensure the app will behave safely and consistently for all users.",
    "Vibe testing alone simply can’t provide that level of confidence, which is why relying on it to launch an LLM application is insufficient."
] 

