    "Vibe testing is the practice of giving an LLM‑based application just a handful of prompts—usually five to ten—reading the answers, and then deciding “by feel” whether the system seems to be working.",
    "It’s an informal, subjective check: you ask a few questions that come to mind, see if the responses look right, and if they do, you conclude the app is fine.",
    "Because the set of prompts is small, chosen ad‑hoc, and the judgment is based on personal impression, the process isn’t repeatable; the next time you might ask completely different questions and get a different sense of quality.",
    "That casual approach is useful only for quick, personal experiments.",
    "When you try to move an LLM app into production, vibe testing falls short for several reasons.",
    "First, because it’s not systematic—you’re not covering the full range of inputs the real users will encounter, so many edge cases and failure modes stay hidden.",
    "Second, the lack of repeatability means you can’t reliably track whether a change in the model or the code has introduced a regression; you have no baseline to compare against.",
    "Third, the subjective “feel” judgment doesn’t give you measurable metrics or evidence that the system meets required performance, safety, or reliability standards.",
    "In an industry setting, production‑grade software needs rigorous, methodological evaluation—structured test suites, quantitative metrics, and repeatable procedures—to ensure it behaves predictably under diverse conditions.",
    "Vibe testing simply doesn’t provide that level of assurance, which is why relying on it alone is insufficient for deploying an LLM application."
] 

