Claims:
[
    "Drift is when any one of the three pieces—data the model was trained on, the way the model interprets that data, or the expectations of the people who use it—changes, breaking the agreement.",
    "Data drift is when the statistical properties of the input data shift over time.",
    "Even if you haven’t deliberately changed anything, the real‑world data that flows into your system can evolve, and the model may start seeing patterns it never saw during training.",
    "Model drift refers to the model itself changing its behavior, perhaps because it’s being updated, fine‑tuned, or because the underlying learning algorithm reacts to new data in unexpected ways.",
    "Concept drift is when the underlying relationship between inputs and the target you care about changes.",
    "For example, a chart that used to show sales trends might now be repurposed for a completely different analysis, so the “meaning” of the data has shifted.",
    "User drift is the change in who is using the system and what they expect from it.",
    "The example in the notes—millennials being replaced by Gen‑Z users—illustrates that even if the model and data stay the same, a new user cohort may have different expectations, leading to dissatisfaction (thumbs‑down feedback) despite the model still performing well on offline metrics.",
    "When you ship a model, you’re no longer in a controlled lab environment.",
    "Your offline evaluation may look perfect, but once real users start interacting, any of the drifts above can cause the model’s performance to degrade.",
    "The system might start giving answers that no longer match what users need, leading to negative feedback, loss of trust, or even business impact.",
    "Drift is the reason why a model that looked great in testing can become unreliable in production if you don’t monitor it.",
    "After deployment you need to keep checking whether the data distribution, the model’s behavior, the underlying concepts, or the user base have shifted.",
    "Detecting and responding to drift is essential to maintain the quality and relevance of your AI system over time."
] 

