    "Thumbs‑up and thumbs‑down buttons are a very simple, immediate “was this answer right?” poll that the user can give while they’re chatting with the bot.",
    "When a user clicks thumbs‑up, you can treat that interaction as a signal that the response was correct and useful; when they click thumbs‑down, it’s a signal that something went wrong—perhaps the answer was wrong, confusing, or otherwise unsatisfactory.",
    "We are already storing a lot of information about each turn—what the user asked, the context the retrieval‑augmented generation (RAG) system pulled, the exact output the model produced, latency, token counts, any error codes, and even extra behavioral cues like “I want to talk to a human” or an email address—so those thumbs‑up/down clicks become an extra label that we can attach to each stored record.",
    "Over time, the collection of interactions that received thumbs‑up can be assembled as positive examples, while the ones with thumbs‑down become negative examples.",
    "When you gather all of those labeled instances together, you end up with a “golden dataset”: a curated set of question‑context‑answer triples that have been verified (or at least strongly hinted at) by real users as correct or incorrect.",
    "In practice, you would filter the stored logs for the interactions that have a thumbs‑up, extract the question, the retrieved context, and the model’s answer, and mark that triple as a high‑quality entry.",
    "Conversely, you might set aside the thumbs‑down cases for further review—either to correct the answer, improve the retrieval step, or simply to flag them as problematic examples.",
    "By repeatedly feeding this user‑validated data back into your evaluation pipeline, you get a continuously improving golden dataset that reflects actual user judgments of correctness, rather than relying solely on synthetic or manually crafted test cases.",
    "This is why the thumbs‑up/down feedback is such a useful signal for building a reliable, user‑grounded benchmark."
] 

